sections.body:
Java evaluates an if/else if/else chain strictly in order, from top to bottom, and executes the block belonging to the **first** condition that evaluates to true, every subsequent else if and else in that same chain is then skipped, even if a later condition would also have been true.

```java
int score = 85;
if (score >= 90) {
    System.out.println("A");
} else if (score >= 80) {
    System.out.println("B");   // this one runs
} else if (score >= 70) {
    System.out.println("C");   // never even checked, because a branch above already matched
} else {
    System.out.println("F");
}
```

Order matters: if the `score >= 70` check were written *above* `score >= 80`, a score of 85 would incorrectly print "C", since that broader condition is also technically true and it would be reached first. A related, easy-to-miss hazard is the so-called dangling else: when an if has no braces and is nested inside another if with no braces, it can become ambiguous to a human reader (though not to the compiler, which always attaches an else to the nearest unmatched if) which if an else belongs to. Put braces `{ }` around every if/else body, even a single statement, and the question goes away.

A classic `switch` compares one value against a series of `case` labels and jumps to the matching one, but execution does not stop there automatically. Without a `break`, control **falls through** into the next case's code, and the one after that, and so on, until it hits a break or runs out of cases. Java inherited this from C. It lets several case labels share one block of code by stacking labels with nothing between them, but it also means a forgotten `break` gives you extra behavior with no error. (Compiling with `javac -Xlint:fallthrough` makes the compiler warn about it; the warning is off by default.)

```java
int day = 3;
switch (day) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("Monday");
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Tuesday");
    case 3:
        System.out.println("Wednesday");
    case 4:
        System.out.println("Thursday");
    default:
        System.out.println("Some other day");
}
```

With `day == 3`, this prints "Wednesday", then falls straight through into "Thursday" and "Some other day" too, because none of those three cases has a `break` stopping it. `default` is optional in this form. Without it, a value matching no case does nothing at all, so it's usually worth adding one that logs or throws.

Java 14 made `switch` usable as an **expression**, one that produces a value you can assign or return, and added the `->` label as an alternative to `:` and `break`. Each arrow case is an independent branch, so there is no fall-through and no `break` to forget.

```java
int day = 3;
String name = switch (day) {
    case 1 -> "Monday";
    case 2 -> "Tuesday";
    case 3 -> "Wednesday";
    default -> "Some other day";
};
System.out.println(name);   // Wednesday, and only Wednesday
```

For a case that needs more than one expression's worth of logic, a braced block after `->` can compute a value and hand it back with `yield`: `case 3 -> { System.out.println("matched three"); yield "Wednesday"; }`. A switch expression must also be exhaustive: leave out `default` here and the code doesn't compile, because some int would have no value. For an enum you can list every constant in place of `default`, and then adding a constant later produces a compile error at each switch that forgot it.

Arrow labels work in switch *statements* too, for branches with side effects. The classic colon-and-break form still works as it always has and you'll meet it in older code, but prefer arrows in new code.

