sections.body:
A method declaration is `modifiers returnType name(parameterList) { body }`, and the compiler checks the return type. `void` means the method hands nothing back to its caller. Any other return type means **every possible path** through the body must `return` a value of that type or throw an exception. If the compiler finds one path that falls off the end, you get a compile-time error, `missing return statement`.

```java
static int classify(int score) {
    if (score >= 60) {
        return 1;
    } else {
        return 0;
    }
    // no missing-return risk here -- both branches of the if/else return
}
```

If that `else` were removed, leaving only the `if`, the compiler would reject the method: there'd be a path (score less than 60) that reaches the end of the method with nothing returned, which isn't allowed for a method declared to return `int`. The compiler doesn't reason about values, though. Write `if (score >= 60) return 1; if (score < 60) return 0;` and it still reports a missing return, because it doesn't notice that the two conditions cover every case.

Java lets several methods share the exact same name as long as their parameter lists differ in type, number, or order. This is called **overloading**, and it's resolved by the compiler, based on the static (declared) types of the arguments at the call site, before the program ever runs.

```java
static int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
static double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
static String add(String a, String b) { return a + b; }

add(2, 3);        // compiler picks the (int, int) overload -> 5
add(2.5, 3.5);     // compiler picks the (double, double) overload -> 6.0
add("2", "3");     // compiler picks the (String, String) overload -> "23"
```

A method that differs from another *only* in its return type, with an identical parameter list, is not a legal overload; the compiler can't disambiguate a call based on what you do with the result, only on the arguments you pass in. Overloading is sometimes called 'compile-time polymorphism' because the choice of overload is fixed when the code is compiled, from declared argument types. *Overriding*, which you'll meet with inheritance, picks the method at run time from the object's type.

An array is created with a size that is fixed permanently at the moment of creation: `int[] scores = new int[5];` allocates room for exactly five `int` values, default-initialized to `0`, and that size can never grow or shrink afterward; there is no 'add one more element' operation on an array itself. Elements are accessed by a zero-based index, `scores[0]` through `scores[4]` for a five-element array, and reading or writing an index outside that range compiles just fine but throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` at run time; the compiler has no way to verify indices against array length in general, since indices are often computed from data that only exists while the program is running.

```java
int[] scores = new int[3];
scores[0] = 90;
scores[1] = 85;
scores[2] = 78;
System.out.println(scores.length);   // 3 -- .length is a FIELD here, not a method call
System.out.println(scores[3]);       // compiles fine, throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException at run time
```

Arrays are reference types, even when their elements are primitives: `scores` itself is a reference to an object living on the heap, not the values, and two arrays with equal contents are not `==`. Note that `array.length` is a field, with no parentheses, while `String.length()` is a method call; mixing them up is a compile error. An array *field* holds `null` until you assign it, and reading `.length` through a `null` reference throws `NullPointerException`. (An unassigned *local* array variable won't compile if you read it, as with any local.)

Java is always pass-by-value. What that 'value' is depends on the parameter's type. For a primitive parameter, the value copied is the primitive value itself. For an array (or any reference type) parameter, the value copied is the **reference** (effectively, the address the variable points to) not the array's contents. So mutating an array's elements inside a method is visible to the caller afterward, but reassigning the parameter variable itself inside the method is not.

```java
static void mutate(int[] arr) {
    arr[0] = 99;                 // mutates the array the CALLER's reference also points to
}

static void reassign(int[] arr) {
    arr = new int[]{1, 2, 3};    // only repoints this method's own LOCAL copy of the reference
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    int[] numbers = {0, 0, 0};
    mutate(numbers);
    System.out.println(numbers[0]);   // 99 -- visible: mutate() wrote through the shared reference

    reassign(numbers);
    System.out.println(numbers[0]);   // still 99 -- reassign() only changed ITS OWN copy of the reference
}
```

`mutate` receives a copy of the reference, but that copy still points at the exact same array object the caller's `numbers` points at, so `arr[0] = 99` writes into that one shared array, and the caller sees it. `reassign` also receives a copy of the reference, but `arr = new int[]{1, 2, 3}` only changes what that local copy of the variable points to: it makes `reassign`'s own `arr` point somewhere new, with zero effect on `main`'s `numbers`, which still points at the original array, untouched by that reassignment. It's the assignment rule for references from the types lesson, applied to a parameter.

examples.code:
public class Calc {
    static int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
    static double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
    static String add(String a, String b) { return a + b; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(add(2, 3));         // 5
        System.out.println(add(2.5, 3.5));      // 6.0
        System.out.println(add("2", "3"));      // 23
    }
}

public class Widening {
    static void show(long value) {
        System.out.println("long: " + value);
    }

    static void show(Integer value) {
        System.out.println("Integer: " + value);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        show(5);   // prints "long: 5"
    }
}

public class ArrayPassing {
    static void mutate(int[] arr) {
        arr[0] = 99;
    }

    static void reassign(int[] arr) {
        arr = new int[]{1, 2, 3};
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = {0, 0, 0};

        mutate(numbers);
        System.out.println(numbers[0]);   // 99

        reassign(numbers);
        System.out.println(numbers[0]);   // still 99
    }
}

